    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"12.1 Linear Regression and Classification\"\nicon: \"file-slides\"\nformat:\n  revealjs:\n    theme: [default, slides.scss]\n    scrollable: true\n    revealjs-plugins:\n      - \"fix-plotly-size\"\nexecute: \n  echo: true\n---\n\n::: {#125fc0d1 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.compose import make_column_transformer, make_column_selector\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression, LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score, mean_absolute_error, mean_absolute_percentage_error\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder, StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nimport plotly.express as px\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport plotly.io as pio\npio.templates.default = \"plotly_white\"\npd.options.plotting.backend = \"plotly\"\n```\n:::\n\n\n## Objectives\n\n- explain how scaling features affects a linear regression coefficients\n- preprocess data to prepare it for modeling\n- explain how to use a linear model to make classifications\n\n## Example: Ames Housing again\n\nData link: [ames_home_sales.csv](https://calvin-data-science.github.io/data202/data/ames/ames_home_sales.csv)\n\n\n\n```python\ndata = pd.read_csv(\"data/ames_home_sales.csv\")\n```\n\n::: {#359ab599 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ndata['sale_price'] = data.eval(\"Sale_Price / 1000\")\names = data.query('Gr_Liv_Area < 4000 and Sale_Condition == \"Normal\"')\n```\n:::\n\n\n::: {#190c82ac .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\names_train, ames_test = train_test_split(ames, random_state=42, test_size=0.2)\nprint(f\"Training set size: {len(ames_train)} homes\")\nprint(f\"Test set size: {len(ames_test)} homes\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\nTraining set size: 1929 homes\nTest set size: 483 homes\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#0d99c2ae .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ndef evaluate(y_true, y_pred):\n    return pd.Series({\n        'MAE': mean_absolute_error(y_true, y_pred),\n        'MAPE': mean_absolute_percentage_error(y_true, y_pred),\n    })\n```\n:::\n\n\n## Fit linreg with all numeric features {.smaller}\n\n::: {#daf59156 .cell execution_count=6}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ntarget_column = 'sale_price'\nfeature_columns = [column for column in ames.columns if column != target_column]\nprint(f\"Number of features: {len(feature_columns)}\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\nNumber of features: 74\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#5a9082ca .cell execution_count=7}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ntransformer = make_column_transformer(\n    ('passthrough', make_column_selector(dtype_include=np.number)),\n).set_output(transform='pandas')\ntransformer.fit_transform(ames_train[feature_columns])\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=7}\n```{=html}\n<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>passthrough__Lot_Frontage</th>\n      <th>passthrough__Lot_Area</th>\n      <th>passthrough__Year_Built</th>\n      <th>passthrough__Year_Remod_Add</th>\n      <th>passthrough__Mas_Vnr_Area</th>\n      <th>passthrough__BsmtFin_SF_1</th>\n      <th>passthrough__BsmtFin_SF_2</th>\n      <th>passthrough__Bsmt_Unf_SF</th>\n      <th>passthrough__Total_Bsmt_SF</th>\n      <th>passthrough__First_Flr_SF</th>\n      <th>...</th>\n      <th>passthrough__Enclosed_Porch</th>\n      <th>passthrough__Three_season_porch</th>\n      <th>passthrough__Screen_Porch</th>\n      <th>passthrough__Pool_Area</th>\n      <th>passthrough__Misc_Val</th>\n      <th>passthrough__Mo_Sold</th